<h1>CloudWatch GetMetricData (EC2 utilisation)</h1>
<p class="fine">aws &middot; feeds <b>utilisation</b> &middot;
  <span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
  
  <span class="chip st-blocked">metered</span>
  
</p>


<section class="panel">
  <h2>What it costs to run</h2>
  <p>Billed per 1,000 metrics REQUESTED, not per call, and explicitly excluded from the free tier&#39;s million requests. Memory needs the CloudWatch Agent, whose custom metrics cost from USD 0.30 per metric per month. This is the one connector where a careless loop is genuinely expensive.</p>
  <p class="fine">CPU, network and disk come free at 5-minute granularity with no agent. There is NO memory or disk-space metric by default: that is the whole reason a utilisation feed has coverage below 100% and says so.</p>
  <p class="fine">Authentication: IAM principal with cloudwatch:GetMetricData.
    <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/APIReference/API_GetMetricData.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Provider documentation</a></p>
</section>




<section class="panel">
  <h2>How to get the file</h2>
  <ol class="rules"><li>This one is genuinely metered: billed per THOUSAND METRICS REQUESTED and excluded from the free tier. Ask for the metrics you need and no more.</li><li>Run it once yourself, then upload the JSON.</li></ol>
  
  <p class="fine">If you would rather do it from a terminal:</p>
  <pre class="jd">aws cloudwatch get-metric-data --start-time <TS>Z \
  --end-time <TS>Z --metric-data-queries file://queries.json \
  &gt; cloudwatch.json</pre>
  
</section>




<section class="panel">
  <h2>1. Give it the data - the short way</h2>
  <p class="fine">Drop the file straight in. A zip of the whole export folder is
  fine and is unpacked here; a single CSV or JSON is fine too. Nothing to type,
  no terminal, and it works the same whether it came from AWS, Azure, GCP or a
  cluster of your own. Up to 200 MB.</p>
  <form method="post" action="/connectors/aws-cloudwatch-utilisation/upload" enctype="multipart/form-data"><input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="<CSRF>">
    <input type="file" name="file" class="sel" required
           accept=".zip,.csv,.gz,.json,.parquet">
    <div class="dlgacts" style="justify-content:flex-start;margin-top:10px">
      <button class="btn">Upload and check</button>
    </div>
  </form>
</section>


<section class="panel">
  <h2>1b. Or point at a folder already on this machine</h2>
